i am very sorry about cowboy not getting  another season  but  uh but you've landed on your feet  getting to work with a coen brother and  macbeth so let's you know i think i  think you're good  so i have a lot of questions um i when i  was researching preparing to talk to you  i read that one like your first movie  role was in cold mountain as like an  orderly if i'm not mistaken um what was  it like uh what do you remember about  making that movie and getting to be in  like a big movie like that  i largely remember really embarrassing  myself in a bunch of different ways i'd  never been on a film set well i'd hardly  been on a set at all but certainly  nothing of that magnitude  and um one of my  abiding memories  was that i had to play an orderly and i  had to carry someone on a stretcher in a  really important sort of sad scene with  jude law and lucas black  and we had to be walking up a  steep incline all day and i had his head  at my end tonight and i'm not a  particularly big guy i was much less  than  less big then and there were all these  extras that were army constricts scripts  doing the same thing over and over  anyway at the end of the day i just  couldn't hold this this um stretcher  anymore but i didn't want to say  anything i didn't want to sort of expose  it i was weak so in a shot i just it  slipped out of my hands and lucas fell  to the floor and the stretcher snapped  in half he landed on his mic there's  this horrifying silence and i just went  sorry sorry sorry sorry  and they cut and i was just really  really embarrassed and then and then a  couple of days later i was talking you  know very lucky enough to be in the  company of jude law and anthony miguela  and i think maybe even sydney pollock  was there at the time too and we were  covered in blood fake blood which is  made of sugar and a wasp got into my ear  but no one saw this wasp get into my ear  so in the middle of the conversation i  was just like freaking out like this it  was terrible i mean largely i just  embarrassed myself  hopefully i've got better on sets since  then but it wasn't a great start  uh thank you for sharing those that's  some great stuff um obviously like like  like everyone you're a fan of the coen  brothers and joel cohen and i'm just  curious what was it like actually  sitting down with him for the first time  getting how did you get cast in this and  was a little bit nerve-wracking sitting  across from someone who's such a gifted  filmmaker  what is so amazing about joel is that  he's incredibly personable so any um  sort of  nerves and excitement i mean great  excitement on my part but nerves  instantly kind of disappear because he's  just such a like a down-to-earth normal  guy  he just has a great deal of experience  genius  and more talent than um  many people put together but he makes  you feel very at home and i think that's  part of his gift as a director is that  he relaxes you without even trying i  think and it means that you  explore  further reaches of a character or of an  idea of a scene than you might with  other directors because you feel very  held by him  in terms of being cast  i was lucky enough to be cast by ellen  chenoweth who cast this in a film called  suburbicon  so she knew my work and she'd seen me as  henry the fifth in fact so i think she  must have remembered that i did  shakespeare and i auditioned for harry  melling's part in um the ballad of  buster scruggs doing a bit of  shakespeare on tape so i maybe you know  she remembered that too  and i just basically made a tape  for joel and um and i don't really know  why me as opposed to anyone else but i'm  very pleased that um  that was the case they couldn't find  anyone else to fit into the costume  maybe i'm not sure  i think it could be you may you might  have some talent you know just a little  um i try  uh what was your reaction though  stepping on set for the first time and  seeing  the the uh the fact that it was all  gonna be on sound stages that he was  gonna be filming in black and white you  know the aesthetical the aesthetic  choices he made for the production  it was all just immensely exciting and  they they  it all just made sense  when you  i mean he's created a real end to end  realized visual world and kind of  approach in this film and that is very  rare i've not been part of something  really that  is so kind of hermetically sealed in its  own thing in the way that this film is  um and it just makes knowing the play  very well i've directed the play and  know very well it just makes perfect  sense for this for this story um  and  i  you you get a sense on set often that  something is interesting um but you  never know how it's going to turn out  but it really just everything i saw was  was made me very excited and hopeful  about what it'd be like when it came  together and then  when i you know when i saw the whole  thing as an audience member and as a  cohen's fan as a fan of cinema and of  shakespeare i think it's a really  brilliant version of this film and a  really brilliant film you know i'm very  very honoured and grateful to be part of  it  i loved bruno cinematography and at  times it reminded me a little bit of  like german expressionism you know with  the way it was shot can you sort of talk  about working with bruno  bruno also is a lovely man i mean this  is the thing when you get to work with  people at this level they you know joel  they've collected  the people that they like to work with  most and um  bruno is a very collaborative open warm  person too and works very very hard we  moved very fast we didn't have very long  to shoot this um so we shot a lot of  setups in the day  so you know bruno really had to run  around  um he never  sort of told us what to do even though  you could tell that  it was a very pictorial composed you  know visual piece  um  and i just think he's done amazing work  as you say german expressionism bergman  um kurosawa uh there are so many amazing  references what i love about the film is  it it's sort of set in the history of  shakespearean cinema in a weird way um  that i think is  makes it even more otherworldly  what do you think it is  i'm just curious shakespeare has  obviously been adapted and re-adapted in  so many different versions what do you  think it is about shakespeare's writing  that has resonated with people for  hundreds of years and everyone keeps  going back to it  i think there's a number of factors i  think he writes or sort of pillaged  really really good stories  but then  he  is just so perceptive about human nature  i think um  about people's wants and desires about  the underbelly of  uh of  those desires i think what one it might  be willing to do  for what you think is a good thing or  something that you think is important to  you  and  then he  writes those stories and those ideas in  such incredibly dense  language that can be  interpretable  infinitely in that it's so complicated  that you can't land on the right way of  doing it or  one way of doing it it's too big and  therefore you can as an actor you can in  the same part over hundreds of  performances find new stuff in it even  as the same actor let alone a bunch of  different actors playing the same part  that note i gotta stop i'm just gonna  say congrats on being part of this film  and getting to work with you know uh  joel and everybody else and i wish you  nothing but the best  thanks man hope to see you again really  soon i hope so as well thank you so much  for your time man
 

